// readfs.capa
//
// The central Capa property of LicenseAudit: the auditor reads the
// repository it audits through a capability that declares only `read`.
//
// `ReadOnlyFs` is a user-defined capability that exposes exactly one
// method, `read`. It has no `write`, no `remove`, no mutating operation of
// any kind. Every part of LicenseAudit that touches the audited repo takes
// a `ReadOnlyFs`, never a raw `Fs`. Because a capability's surface is its
// method set, the compiler refuses a call such as `ro.write(...)` on a
// `ReadOnlyFs`: it is not a runtime failure, it does not type-check.
// leaky_licenseaudit.capa is the counter-example that makes the compiler
// say so.
//
// The bridge from the real filesystem to this read-only view is
// `make_read_view`: it takes the built-in `Fs`, narrows it to a single
// directory prefix with `restrict_to`, and returns the implementor,
// `FsReadView`, which keeps the narrowed `Fs` in a field. Two
// narrowings apply to the audited tree: path attenuation (the view is
// restricted to the audited dir) and method attenuation (the
// `ReadOnlyFs` capability declares only `read`; the reports are
// written through a separate `Fs`).

// The abstract read-only filesystem capability. One method, read; nothing
// that mutates. This is the whole authority the auditor holds over the
// repository under audit.
pub capability ReadOnlyFs
    fun read(self, path: String) -> Result<String, IoError>

// The concrete implementor. It holds a built-in `Fs` in a field: the
// cap-bearing-struct relaxation lets a struct that implements a
// user-defined capability hold built-in capabilities other than
// `Unsafe` as bare fields.
type FsReadView {
    fs: Fs
}

impl ReadOnlyFs for FsReadView
    fun read(self, path: String) -> Result<String, IoError>
        return self.fs.read(path)

// The bridge: narrow the real `Fs` to a directory prefix and wrap it in
// the read-only view.
pub fun make_read_view(fs: Fs, prefix: String) -> FsReadView
    return FsReadView { fs: fs.restrict_to(prefix) }
